- NOTE -
Spoilers! What follows also probably isn't meticulously accurate move
for move, because I wasn't keeping track of what occured. This is more
of an impressionistic pastiche of events to give a flavor of my
favorite bones experience.

Bryhnhildr the Valkyrie

The game started unremarkably, minetown was devoid of anything useful,
left there with dwarf mithril and components totaling -7ac. Kept my pet
dog Oski through Sokoban using a magic whistle, until a Troll clocked
her. Got reflection.

Things got weird on level 12 or thereabouts. I had sacrificed several
times at altars in minetown and on level 9, but Odin hadn't seen fit to
grant me a hammer. I was feeling pretty good about my combat skill with
the trusty +1 long sword and a solid 105 HP, so I converted a Chaotic
altar right in front a Priestess of Loki, and she became quite upset.
She summoned some insects, and pounded me solidly with a mace until I
was all but done for. I managed to flee the desecrated (reconsecrated?)
temple, screaming priestess in tow, and I led her on a merry chase
through the dungeon, casting aside ants and receiving a great deal of
psychic trauma from her various nasty chaotic priestly magiks. I do
believe she weakened me on more than one occasion, and put scales on my
eyes. Eventually I managed to lock her in an isolated chamber (full
moon indeed!), and made my way achingly back to the newly minted altar
to Odin. There I sacrificed again amongst the leering ghostly shadows,
a leocrotta, a long worm, a hill giant, and received naught but clover
for my efforts.

So I headed down, and hit the most unpleasent bones level I've
encountered to date. You see, I had recently taken a spin though the
Dungeons of Doom as a monk, and had been doing quite well, when I found
myself unexpectedly polymorphed into a Nalfeshnee. Quite interesting.
My first encounter as the nastiest of major demons was with a small
leprechaun mound and imagine my surprise when I began spontaneously
summoning more nalfeshnees! Help continued to arrive, until there were
seven of us. I named them as I met them, and they gnarled with malice.
It was great fun sweeping the halls with my six aggressively evil
brethren wiping out anything in our path, until I brashly carved my way
into a cockatrice, expecting little resistance, only to realize that my
one of my primary attacks was a bite. A stone dead monk/nalfeshnee was
I and a stupid death at that.

Needless to say the RNG perked right up at this one, and the bones were
quietly stashed away to be cruelly inflicted on the next poor cursed
and deeply wounded adventurer to come wandering along.

Which my Bryhnhildr did some days later, just after the encounter with
the bitchy Lokian, and as I entered level 13, there crouched my former
nalfeshnee compatriots silently lurking in the darkness, contemplating
unspeakable deviltry. They set straight to work on me, maliciously
cursing, summoning, and stunning me with a verve and aplomb befitting
their demonic nature. I felt as though I needed help, and my head ached
painfully. I felt a little hungry, too.

Lazaro, Tourmaline, Tobias, Robard, Creampuff, and one anonymous
nalfeshnee blasted me nigh unto to death, and I fled before them up the
stair to lvl 12 where my Chaotic mistress still paced, trapped in her
prison, waiting for the chance to crack me with that mace. I recovered
from the nalfeshnee ambush, and bravely descended once more, long sword
gleaming. It took four rounds of descent and return, but I eventually
carved my way through the vile servants of Moloch, and sat there with
14 HP, and a pack full of cursed stuff, panting.

As an experienced nethack player, I am confident that my reader will be
able, at this point, to foresee the approaching punch line. A quick
recap: Converted chaotic altar, was all but finished off by a Lokian
Priestess, but survived and beguiled her into a homemade jail cell.
Descended into a pack of six hungry nalfeshnee left from a previous
death due to cockatrice stoning, and barely survived again, but came
through with style and grace, and was now ready to bravely venture
forward to challenge my destiny.

So I put on my blindfold to scan the dungeon for enemies, and then take
one step, two...

"You try to feel what is on the floor here..."
"There is a cockatrice corpse here!"
"Touching the cockatrice corpse was a fatal mistake!"
"Your limbs are growing stiff..."
"You turn to stone."
"Do you want your possessions identified?"

Bones beget bones in the Dungeons of Doom.

